g killed for example) trigger some less-than-diplomatic sound sequences, taken right out of 'Beavis and Butthead'. In case you are offended by that kind of vocabulary, just uncheck this box. Then go ahead and let granny and the kids play.The Game:SpaceBall is designed with two human players in minds (no, there is no computer opponent. Yet). The first thing you'll see when the game starts is the playing field... predictable, wasn't it? Check out the upper corners: Those are the scoring panels. There you will be able to see how many times you have scored, how many lives you have left, the icon for the powerup you are holding (if any) and the status of your shields. Then look at the lower corners: Those are the bases. When you get killed, they'll close up and after a few seconds, expell a brand new ship (as long as you have lives left, of course). Moving up and down the left and right walls are the goals. You should try to make the ball go into your opponent's goal by using any means at your disposal. Also, look at the green glowing bars: Those are the area limits. If you touch your opponent's limit, you'll get kicked back pretty violently and you'll recieve some damage. Do it too many times, and you may find your ship suddenly turns into a nice fireball.When the starting countdown reaches zero, both ships will be expelled from their bases and you will have control of your ship. At the same time, the ball will drop from the ceiling. Both players should now try to catch the ball and score the points needed to win (or kill all of the other player's lives). If you recieve any damage, you will automatically drop the ball, so try to avoid getting hit while you are setting up for a scoring run. The key controls are:Left Player  (Blue)[A]			Go Left[D]			Go Right[W]			Go Up[S]			Go Down[Control]			Shoot/Release Ball[Tab]			Activate Powerup[Shift]			Turn aroundRight Player  (Red)[Left Arrow]			Go Left[Right Arrow]			Go Right[Up Arrow]			Go Up[Down Arrow]			Go Down[Space]			Shoot/Release Ball[Option]			Activate Powerup[Command]			Turn around[P]			Pause and show menubarPowerups:At random times, a yellow/green sphere will appear and start moving around erratically. That is the goodie sphere. If you manage to catch it (not always an easy task) you will randomly get any of five powerups:Missile:	The missile icon looks like, well, a missile. It is small, fast, and guided. It only inflicts a moderate amount of damage, but it is very useful when you are trying to force your opponent to drop that ball.Rocket:	The rocket is solid fuel propelled death. It accelerates very fast and packs such a big punch it will kill your opponent 95% of the times. But it isn't guided, so you'll have a pretty hard time hitting anything that moves. It can be done, though, with a little practice.Magneto:	The icon looks like a magnet next to a ball. When activated, it will attract the ball towards your ship with a huge force. Be careful when you use it, though, because the ball needs to be moving freely for it to work. Otherwise it will just be wasted.Powerball:	This powerup's icon looks like a speeding ball. It will shoot the ball forward at high speed. If you aim it just right, you can score all the way across the playing field. The other player can still catch the ball in mid flight, though. If you activate it when you are not holding the ball, it will be wasted.Instant Healing:	If you grab the goodie containing this one, your ship will sparkle briefly and your shields will go back up to full charge. Very useful at those times when your shields display has absolutely no green in it. As it's name indicates, it is instant. No "saving up for the tough times" with this one.Well, thats pretty much it. Get a friend and go play the game, it is pretty simple and you will figure out anything else on your own.-- Tips/Hints¥ Remember to set the Lives and Points fields in the settings screen to define the kind of match you want. You can always win by killing all of the other player's lives, so at any given time that may be the best option.¥ It is sometimes useful to shoot at the ball. You may force it to go into your opponent's goal, or you may manage to change it's trajectory just before it goes into yours.¥ Keep moving and keep shooting.¥ If possible, play the game with resolutions of 800x600 or higher. Higher resolutions will give you bigger playing fields. But, if you are looking for furious close quarters action, 640x480 can be a good choice.¥ This one comes as a reward for reading the ReadMe file: Some objects in the playing field can be destroyed if you shoot at them for long enough...-- Misc StuffI'm an 18 year old student (like most game programmers ) from Mendoza, Argentina. I started programming this game as an experiment to see if I could put together a game with the little programming I knew. Then it turned into a learning experience, and finally into a project. Did I mention this is my first piece of software ever? Well it is. That should be enough to explain that certain "crappiness" inherent to this game :) Hopefully I'll become better at this "development" thing over the years. Hey, maybe some day you may have to pay to play one of my games...  ;)That's right, this software is freeware. That means you can use it as long as you want without feeling even a little bit of guilt. But, if you play the game and love it (or hate it), you are encouraged to send email to me at:PuppiesOnAcid@softhome.netIf you think there is something that absolutely has to be added/changed, just mail me and tell me. I'll do what I can to make it happen (if I like the idea, thats it). If you get ideas for additional powerups, write to me too. Also, I'm always looking for good ideas for games. -- Compatibility Issues¥ When using Sound Manager versions greater than 3.3, the game becomes a little sluggish when playing sounds. This is Apple's fault, and I think there is nothing I can do right now to fix it. If your Mac is fast enough, you may not notice. If you do notice, the only workaround I could find is installing Sound Manager 3.3 or earlier. That, or getting a faster Mac :)¥ If your mac crashes when the game is starting up, it may have something to do with the menu hiding routines. If you press 'option' while the game is starting up, it will not hide the menu bar. This is more compatible but the game won't look as good with a gray band across the top of the monitor.¥ The higher the resolution you use, the more memory you will need. If you plan to use the game with resolutions above 800x600, increase the preferred RAM size of the application by a couple of megabytes.¥ If you want maximum compatibility, uncheck 'Fast Animation' on the settings dialog (or from the menu bar while paused). This will force the game to draw the animation with slower but safer methods. Again, if your machine is fast enough, you won't notice the difference.¥ The game will start up and run without Quicktime installed, but some graphics may not display properly. You can play like that if you don't mind, but it won't look so good.-- Version History¥ 1.0 -- 25/10/02 -- First Release!-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Legal Stuff: You may not profit from this game without full consent from the author. You may distribute it freely as long as this file remains unchanged and in the same folder as the application. You may not distribute modified versions of the software without full consent from the author.